collection of web design and development news and thoughts from CodePen's o=
wn Chris Coyier.</p>=0D=0A                          </mj-raw>=0D=0A        =
                  <h3>Layers of Layers</h3>=0A<p>There=E2=80=99s this thing=
 that you need to know about when dealing with <code>z-index</code> and try=
ing to get some elements to be on top of other elements: <a href=3D"https:/=
/post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/Gtd5O7Xvsbg1KAd39n0UnA~~/AABEfhA~/dzQ7stJkqUtyO=
ichX6jIaCFSxqGDEapnFRMRCjiZWqypv_5H0rQ8A4ucnki8CkBP6rahr2GZT0jmG17fRNdqzqfr=
NlQfnZ85jwvxR-uzM58uWOhs-xZd_hMA_XHwQkvz">stacking contexts</a>. If you=E2=
=80=99ve got an element within a stacking context that is itself lower than=
 another element that you=E2=80=99re trying to get on top of, well, you=E2=
=80=99re hosed.</p>=0A<p>And yet, these days, there is something of an esca=
pe hatch. Stephanie Eckles <a href=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/ni=
gD8t_GeIzj-66snsmIZA~~/AABEfhA~/Xl1WwHTldMSHG_nre6L9yP75hggirBUhbVV57vVPRGj=
QRae66nrONs5YMI7284ege_zJuz3MiFjd1ByGU8dakBBebmQcxqRN5hXEiVl3L_4twLbl5Ol4ot=
xVMPqd_e61Y73Bbsd8Qd5nZO52eH9xtw~~">introduces our hero</a>:</p>=0A<blockqu=
ote><p>A modern web feature is the =E2=80=9C<a href=3D"https://post.spmailt=
echnol.com/f/a/d4j6ki9E46UOXof-fC-qsw~~/AABEfhA~/UpDHdRxCN-N-ae4tSzR_4HmxWC=
aQisfnReClVsvtq-sGpFzERGNUTHVMWJFIhLhgIROG_Ir5rDIEj48gNYyapcoFLQdd6BAgtR9A8=
QzKBLEpoDl4sQUnCBtWGTFJkqOzGF5J-tDgRna1k8_at4LARcgElcpZdZipuJKo0pqeRDY~">to=
p layer</a>,=E2=80=9D which is guaranteed to be the top-most layer above an=
y other stacking context. It spans the entire viewport, although elements e=
xisting in the top layer may have smaller visible dimensions.</p></blockquo=
te>=0A<p>If you can get an element onto the top layer, it just <em>will</em=
> be on top of everything else, regardless of stacking context. Open <a hre=
f=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/14RB3GAkpfnBgJRk2DTWxw~~/AABEfhA~/8=
XAW0VFq_Gv3ifEhRYF5-lzgUmJfIb8LYPnnhAgO6URFh3ETXNhyO39IzDPfuvSVi1fUO1YZbD1b=
TKFnvORQr81ufmOud4PSUNtchMQLLeAFnKCfNEcHpChkOS1vHgy4rf-SZTHsYt0yPkuzacgtu2z=
8BlhzKjy5-aRFjqlYnfk3lV66_QdWo4Bw_uxUEFkamcPv0fmoKUYQ6gMAIVD-zg~~">dialogs =
and popovers</a> will be on the top layer automatically.</p>=0A<p>And yet.<=
/p>=0A<p>This combination of dialog and popover can potentially lead to oth=
er stacking problems where a visual popover might still be rendered inacces=
sible by a dialog. This is the whole thing <a href=3D"https://post.spmailte=
chnol.com/f/a/nigD8t_GeIzj-66snsmIZA~~/AABEfhA~/Xl1WwHTldMSHG_nre6L9yP75hgg=
irBUhbVV57vVPRGjQRae66nrONs5YMI7284ege_zJuz3MiFjd1ByGU8dakBBebmQcxqRN5hXEiV=
l3L_4twLbl5Ol4otxVMPqd_e61Y73Bbsd8Qd5nZO52eH9xtw~~">Stephanie is talking ab=
out</a>.</p>=0A<blockquote><p>When the native HTML dialog is launched via <=
code>showModal()</code>, the page outside of the dialog becomes <em>inert</=
em>. The state of <em>inert</em> is a necessary accessibility behavior, whi=
ch results in isolating the dialog contents, and prevents both tab and virt=
ual cursor access to the background page.</p></blockquote>=0A<p>A popover i=
s part of the background page, so even if you can see it, when a modal is o=
pen, it=E2=80=99s inert. Weird. Quite the gotcha.</p>=0A<p>Speaking of the =
top layer, I finished up a three-part series call In-N-Out Animations (<a h=
ref=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/bwr4zMoyNPSEO15mqciQRw~~/AABEfhA~=
/vXDAkJYKwbIWqV3eLAWmHT0XS4crvTyoJ9JYpH32k1NW7piD8UDU968tfaB3g5SeY9foqpd3VC=
cY459VDVrzva7YJDJsO7am63AEMNms5pjNXJxfXSuxIdZfe5tRSDJZH9Kyfjnv8dTi38C4I8WKu=
9qJ77F0uMQcUKB0LccaILM~">starting here</a>) that goes all into animating di=
alogs and popover both on the way in and on the way out.</p>=0A<p>Don=E2=80=
=99t confuse the =E2=80=9Ctop layer=E2=80=9D with <code>@layer</code> in CS=
S. Different concepts. Using <code>@layer</code> in CSS allows you to make =
whole chunks of CSS apply as more-strong or less-strong than other chunks o=
f CSS. Beating even specificity, which just feels <em>extra</em> weird. I=
=E2=80=99d tell you it=E2=80=99s kinda like <code>z-index</code> for actual=
 CSS code but, uhm, that=E2=80=99s going to confuse you so pretend I didn=
=E2=80=99t say that.</p>=0A<p>Imagine you have:</p>=0A<pre><code class=3D"h=
tml">&lt;div class=3D&quot;el&quot; id=3D&quot;el&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;=0A<=
/code></pre>=0A<p>Certainly an <code>#el</code> selector is going to beat a=
n <code>.el</code> selector, because that=E2=80=99s just how CSS specificit=
y works. But if we layer it, we can <a href=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol.c=
om/f/a/BPLG24Eq6ltqNwlTpKt4BA~~/AABEfhA~/nu_ybx4D2M4nZPA5uavkysPCpbIUQt0ege=
UQK3IaTyY6i8QTXt_I5YL4bbFwDSnyemOix6tdwDRv2Mk-RwOz_qyqTJUffvBY-haBKg0ejURm7=
vPT4vcXTfLyMxt7yBLwECStSNYNbuZND3ZEQhZqZXhJvqfftYDLh7aegGYzfP6E5XTpdqiZlB9x=
XkWLwJYD">make the class selector win instead</a>.</p>=0A<pre><code class=
=3D"css">@layer my-layer-one, my-layer-two;=0A=0A@layer my-layer-one {=0A=
=09#el {=0A=09=09background: red;=0A=09}=0A}=0A=0A@layer my-layer-two {=0A=
=09.el {=0A=09=09background: green;=0A=09}=0A}=0A</code></pre>=0A<p>Sprinkl=
ing on another little bit of knowledge, styles that <em>aren=E2=80=99t in a=
ny layer</em> are stronger than any layered style. So if you want to <em>lo=
wer</em> the strength of a particular chunk of CSS, just layer it. <a href=
=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/hy09g72C42dII7mejHfeWA~~/AABEfhA~/lp=
mmIeb_JKLFUUWMSrG9XuNV434VuN1UGsB1w38zx9iTSo62eWOxxapOUL6pS_zs7vPYfr4Leeoc3=
xFEDGmkuTGAUrpeUGi_yb5zVbCwbt9zO5QIciWvOOsC5-xIROpNgPHclaH9vXd3mNqy_PnrJ4oQ=
uwECF_ew3pjVZOXR0uqvvvl5Vr0QSBpMxVYUh_fz">Manuel Matuzo shared this idea</a=
>, as an alternative to the <code>:where()</code> trick you see sometimes.<=
/p>=0A<p>Layering CSS is kind of a big choice. It generally makes more sens=
e to me as something you=E2=80=99d do from the outset of a project. But mos=
t of us aren=E2=80=99t working on brand new CSS architectures every day. Is=
 it possible to sprinkle in layers when useful=3F It clearly was for Manuel=
, and honestly, we do it here at CodePen in an interesting way I=E2=80=99ll=
 have to share sometime. But also, Victor Ayomipo has some thoughts in <a h=
ref=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/2U1eKQuqf8Xks3FsNkwxug~~/AABEfhA~=
/aC7bmgGrLzirYIzdnhRTAi0RKd3VzRfL7CwJ-cOjnMfGMjS_R2lStGZ7wBrrHf8BpfBkY06x6q=
j9mNUIeIoq38jnL1zsulxo6pxSRppfx9R2FJvmSmAZcfDHQ-jf9Vml-2jdMNa5rRF_nf95Ck7Un=
G19k-8CfBbZlbGwLAZcuJb3t21n_wYj2cLlIpDjjAZLSge5i0FgknWG_Y96d1vsSw~~">Integr=
ating CSS Cascade Layers To An Existing Project</a>. A good start is probab=
ly that <a href=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/DxvIsiekXBli_MfryttM3=
w~~/AABEfhA~/ZxpUYpPsYx_JCMqHX-diOjM3PjoIw5YilYASno1YwPk1rdukAKYIH6Cw-W1rGB=
DcN00eqSJghBBxTsDmq_I8UvjJp014FxoaW06jNgOKxTllLobFJKrMLTCOEl2GFkK42WoWvRyqv=
8657olHaRz6nA~~">Your CSS reset should be layered</a>.</p>=0A<hr />=0A<p>Ka=
rl Koch has a nice article <a href=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/iK=
OtHQzo28N88srDXcDmHQ~~/AABEfhA~/apapG-tGZjAkJ6Glw8Pug2_6PktdizDcjwKAlaQcA6B=
hTZwVigdgvzBBFj27csJsmdpK9rZFOAOxHeo3w8APRpcC5xN7RPqt4xCUix5ZexhhNqdto2blYE=
LnVcn9I9fKIvRT8MJ6kZlHTh3EUg5NjyYr3eZcx7KC1a5yv9HqFsE~">On clip-path animat=
ions</a>. The idea is this + button in a corner of a card that, when clicke=
d open, expands into a details area. But the expansion happens by a growing=
 expansion of a clipped circle, which leads to a nice effect.</p>=0A<image =
src=3D"https://production-codepen-email-assets.codepenassets.com/cdn-cgi/im=
age/width=3D600,quality=3D90,format=3Dauto/codepen-spark/radius_card_demo.p=
ng" alt=3D"A modal expanding outward from a small button with a plus symbol=
 on it at the bottom left corner of a page.  " width=3D"400"/>=0A<p>I gotta=
 imagine the new <code>border-shape</code> is going to lead to some super s=
weet animation potential as well.</p>=0A<hr />=0A<p>I think it=E2=80=99s hi=
larious that Temani put together, literally, <a href=3D"https://post.spmail=
technol.com/f/a/AihrpAWLuhsttZNbKw6CiA~~/AABEfhA~/o-HdC6Ug-OMz9uzXld-rh-6cV=
AVir9ZqxbRBeCIiKVyVMTLP4J3gcQzJtvNfc3PC4UMxzGf9VU7jQaRpltIhC0GorwQAiPgLTjWu=
UrDUzvH5qZXa2w7S282Bku8sIvAATZzxfdeS4ET5ujeY_J77Xg~~">one hundred CSS decla=
rations that center an element inside a container</a>.</p>=0D=0A           =
